// The meshsolve package: the numerics that mesh operators solve with. A program
// reaches it with 'import meshsolve', and gets:
//
// - SparseMatrix (sparse.cone): a square f64 matrix in compressed rows, the
//   columns of each row ascending, made by 'fromTriplets' (entries naming one
//   cell are summed in the order given), read with 'at', applied with 'times'.
// - conjugateGradient and SolveReport (solve.cone): the Jacobi-preconditioned
//   conjugate-gradient method on a stored symmetric positive-definite matrix, f64,
//   from the start the caller gives; the report says how many iterations, the
//   relative residual and whether it reached the tolerance.
// - cotangentLaplacian and vertexAreas (laplacian.cone): the cotangent weights
//   of a PolyMesh (each face cut into triangles as PolyMesh.cutFace cuts it) as
//   a matrix with rows summing to zero, and the area round each vertex.
//
// Everything runs in a fixed order in f64, so the same input gives the same bits
// on every machine. 'sculpt' uses it for fairRegion. The sign convention is the
// usual one: (L x)_i = sum over neighbours j of w_ij (x_j - x_i), so L is negative
// semi-definite and -L is the matrix a solve wants.

mod meshsolve;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use *;
